      "provenance": {
        "notes": "A biennial usually grown as an annual: in the second year it flowers and the leaves lose flavour (NC State, RHS); it dies down in winter outdoors, and pots can be brought indoors. NC State warns it is confused with poison hemlock (Conium maculatum). Size: NC State 8 in to 3 ft by 8 in to 1 ft (year-two size stored), RHS 30 to 60 cm. Toxicity: ASPCA lists parsley as toxic to dogs and cats (furanocoumarins, photosensitisation after large amounts); NC State rates humans low severity with the same agent. Graded 1 by the agent; the photosensitisation is stored as skin_irritation, the nearest symptom in our list. Humans eat it, so no child line. The Penn State herb page was not used (not about parsley)."
      },
